{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>On account of the money returned to our sacks.</b> They said: it is not the way of those coming to Egypt from other lands to buy grain and lodge in the house of Joseph, but rather in other places of the city and in inns. And the fact that we are being brought to this house can only be to roll ourselves upon us and fall upon us — that is, that they will bring false charges against us; and thus its Targum renders לאסתקפא, as the Targum of (Deuteronomy 22:14): ״עלילות דברים תסקופי מלין״." "<b>And take us for slaves, and our donkeys.</b> For they were anxious about their donkeys, because the beast on the road is the life of the man, all the more so when they cannot send grain home to their houses and they fear they may die of hunger. And similar to this is (Exodus 17:3): ״להמית אותי ואת בני ואת מקני בצמא״ — He mentioned their beasts together with them, because the beasts were their lives in the wilderness."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {0 "The Targumic term לאסתקפא (from סכפא = false accusation) is left in Aramaic as Rabbeinu Bahya cites it, matching his reference to Onkelos on Deuteronomy 22:14."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:04.010417770Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}